  "/research/": {
    "route": "/research/",
    "file": "research/index.html",
    "title": "Research and Method | MIZAN",
    "description": "Research and method for evidence-backed institutional AI approval decisions.",
    "ogTitle": "Research and Method | MIZAN",
    "ogDescription": "Research and method for evidence-backed institutional AI approval decisions.",
    "canonical": "https://mizangov.com/research/",
    "bodyClass": "",
    "mainHtml": "<section class=\"page-hero\"><div class=\"container narrow\"><div class=\"eyebrow\">Research and method</div><h1>A practical method for evidence-backed AI decisions</h1><p class=\"support\">Our research asks how institutions can connect AI evidence to a clear human decision, sustain supervision, and recognise when an approval needs review. The method is being developed and tested against specific institutional use cases.</p></div></section>\n      <section class=\"section\" aria-labelledby=\"notes-title\"><div class=\"container prose\"><div class=\"section-kicker\">Initial notes</div><h2 id=\"notes-title\" class=\"section-heading medium\">Three method notes for early readers</h2><div class=\"grid-3\" style=\"margin-top:28px\"><article class=\"note-card\" id=\"approval-record\"><h3>What an AI approval record should contain</h3><p>Author: MIZAN research team. Date: 1 October 2026. Distinguishes proposed record fields from findings still being tested.</p></article><article class=\"note-card\" id=\"review-trigger\"><h3>When an approval needs review</h3><p>Author: MIZAN research team. Date: 1 October 2026. Sets out change triggers such as new permissions, model changes and complaints.</p></article><article class=\"note-card\" id=\"capability-test\"><h3>How to test institutional capability for one AI use</h3><p>Author: MIZAN research team. Date: 1 October 2026. Frames capability as use-specific evidence, not a maturity score.</p></article></div></div></section>\n      <section class=\"section light\" aria-labelledby=\"evidence-title\"><div class=\"container prose\"><div class=\"section-kicker\">What the evidence says</div><h2 id=\"evidence-title\" class=\"section-heading medium\">Public guidance converges on named accountability, bounded permissions and review</h2><p>Public bodies researching AI governance, among them the World Bank, the OECD, the UK government and Canada's federal AI guidance, converge on the same requirements: named accountability, bounded permissions, ongoing monitoring, and the ability to pause or withdraw an AI use. MIZAN's method is designed around that convergence.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Cited for context only; no endorsement, certification or formal partnership is implied.</p></div></section>\n      <section class=\"section\" aria-labelledby=\"watch-title\"><div class=\"container\"><div class=\"section-kicker\">Assurance Watch</div><h2 id=\"watch-title\" class=\"section-heading medium\">Assurance Watch</h2><p class=\"section-lede\">A manually maintained, reverse-chronological editorial log of developments in institutional and agentic AI assurance. This is not a live feed and no endorsement is implied.</p><div class=\"watch-list\"><article class=\"watch-entry\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">25 Sep 2026</time><h3>OECD, Governing with Agentic AI in Government</h3><p>Argues that governance cannot rely only on broad principles or after-the-fact review alone, and documents real public-sector approaches to authorising agentic AI.</p><p><strong>What it means for MIZAN:</strong> Supports continuous, built-in authorisation rather than a one-off sign-off.</p><p><a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/governing-with-agentic-ai-when-machines-act-on-governments-behalf\">Primary source</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">Cited for context only; no endorsement, certification or formal partnership is implied.</p></article><article class=\"watch-entry\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">15 Sep 2026</time><h3>Canada, Guide on the Use of Agentic Artificial Intelligence</h3><p>Requires bounded autonomy, designated accountable owners, logging and recoverability, and names automation drift as a risk to monitor.</p><p><strong>What it means for MIZAN:</strong> Close correspondence to MIZAN's boundary and named-holder design.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/responsible-use-ai/generative-ai/guide-use-agentic-artificial-antelligence.html\">Primary source</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">Cited for context only; no endorsement, certification or formal partnership is implied.</p></article><article class=\"watch-entry\"><time datetime=\"2026-04-01\">1 Apr 2026</time><h3>UK, Digital Assurance Playbook and Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard</h3><p>Moves departmental digital and AI assurance toward owned, published records.</p><p><strong>What it means for MIZAN:</strong> MIZAN is designed to reuse ATRS-style records as evidence, not duplicate them.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/digital-assurance-playbook\">Digital Assurance Playbook</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guidance-for-organisations-using-the-algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-guidance-for-public-sector-bodies\">ATRS guidance</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">Cited for context only; no endorsement, certification or formal partnership is implied.</p></article><article class=\"watch-entry\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-01\">2026</time><h3>BCS-led AI assurance consortium</h3><p>A UK government-convened programme on professional competence, ethics and information access for AI assurance.</p><p><strong>What it means for MIZAN:</strong> Relevant to Mu'tamad's question of reviewer and supervisor competence.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.bcs.org/articles-opinion-and-research/government-launches-new-consortium-to-build-trust-in-ai-and-grow-uk-assurance-market\">Primary source</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">Cited for context only; no endorsement, certification or formal partnership is implied.</p></article><article class=\"watch-entry\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-01\">1 Sep 2026</time><h3>NPL, Call for Evidence on AI testing, evaluation and assurance</h3><p>A measurement-focused programme for AI testing, evaluation and assurance in the UK.</p><p><strong>What it means for MIZAN:</strong> Relevant to evidence quality, not to institutional authorisation directly.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.npl.co.uk/research/data-science-and-ai/call-for-evidence-ai-testing-evaluation-and-assurance-in-the-uk\">Primary source</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">Cited for context only; no endorsement, certification or formal partnership is implied.</p></article></div></div></section>\n      <section class=\"section light\" aria-labelledby=\"attribution-title\"><div class=\"container prose\"><div class=\"section-kicker\">Research attribution</div><h2 id=\"attribution-title\" class=\"section-heading medium\">Research context is not product endorsement</h2><p>The founder's UCL research can inform the product thesis. Public guidance from the World Bank, OECD, UK and Canada can inform requirements. Neither constitutes product endorsement. BCS and NPL pathways need precise, dated descriptions of their actual status before use.</p></div></section>"
  },
